---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { site } from '../config';

const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Article',
    headline: 'Coffee Brewing Science & SCA Golden Cup Standard',
    description: 'Detailed explanation of extraction physics, coffee grounds water retention, and the SCA Golden Cup Standard brew ratios.',
    author: {
      '@type': 'Organization',
      name: site.author,
    },
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`Brewing Science & SCA Methodology | ${site.name}`}
  description="Explore the science of coffee extraction, SCA Golden Cup brew ratios, grounds absorption physics, and pulse pouring dynamics."
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <div class="wrap" style="padding: 48px 20px 80px; max-width: 820px;">
    <div class="hero-badge">🔬 Extraction Science</div>
    <h1 style="font-size: 2.4rem; font-weight: 800; color: var(--color-espresso); line-height: 1.2; margin-bottom: 24px;">
      Brewing Methodology & SCA Golden Cup Standard
    </h1>

    <div style="color: var(--color-text-muted); font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.7; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 24px;">
      <section class="card">
        <h2 style="font-size: 1.35rem; font-weight: 800; color: var(--color-espresso); margin-bottom: 12px;">
          1. What is the SCA Golden Cup Standard?
        </h2>
        <p>
          The Specialty Coffee Association (SCA) defines a "Golden Cup" as coffee brewed within precise scientific boundaries for optimal flavor clarity and balance:
        </p>
        <ul style="margin-left: 20px; margin-top: 8px; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 6px;">
          <li><strong>Total Dissolved Solids (TDS):</strong> 1.15% to 1.45% soluble coffee solids in water.</li>
          <li><strong>Extraction Yield:</strong> 18.0% to 22.0% solubles dissolved from the dry coffee grounds.</li>
          <li><strong>Standard Brew Ratio:</strong> 55g/L to 60g/L of water (approximately 1:16 to 1:18, or ~2 tbsp per 6 oz cup).</li>
          <li><strong>Water Temperature:</strong> 195°F – 205°F (90.5°C – 96°C) at contact with the grounds bed.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="card">
        <h2 style="font-size: 1.35rem; font-weight: 800; color: var(--color-espresso); margin-bottom: 12px;">
          2. The Physics of Grounds Absorption
        </h2>
        <p>
          A common home brewing mistake is assuming that water poured equals liquid coffee in your mug.
        </p>
        <p style="margin-top: 8px;">
          Roasted coffee grounds are porous cellular matrices. During extraction, spent coffee grounds retain approximately <strong>2.0g to 2.1g of water per 1.0g of dry coffee</strong>.
        </p>
        <div style="background-color: var(--color-surface-subtle); border-left: 4px solid var(--color-crema); padding: 14px 18px; border-radius: 4px; margin-top: 10px; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.95rem;">
          Final Beverage Yield = Total Water Poured - (Dry Coffee Dose × 2.1)
        </div>
        <p style="margin-top: 10px;">
          When selecting <em>"By Beverage Yield"</em> on BrewRatio, the engine reverses this equation so you brew the exact liquid volume intended for your favorite cup or travel thermos.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="card">
        <h2 style="font-size: 1.35rem; font-weight: 800; color: var(--color-espresso); margin-bottom: 12px;">
          3. Pulse Pouring Dynamics & The 4:6 Method
        </h2>
        <p>
          Pouring all brew water at once often creates channeling and uneven slurry cooling. Segmented pulse pouring unlocks layered flavor profiles:
        </p>
        <ul style="margin-left: 20px; margin-top: 8px; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 8px;">
          <li><strong>The Bloom (Pre-wetting):</strong> Saturating grounds with 2x to 3x coffee weight for 30–45 seconds releases carbon dioxide gas, allowing water to permeate deeply.</li>
          <li><strong>SCA 3-Stage Pour:</strong> Bloom (20%) → Stage 1 (40%) → Stage 2 (40%) balances thermal stability with gentle agitation.</li>
          <li><strong>Tetsu Kasuya 4:6 Method:</strong> World Brewers Cup Champion technique separating the first 40% (governing acidity vs. sweetness) from the remaining 60% (regulating strength and body).</li>
        </ul>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
